true and how he had gone  to Israel and researched it for a number  of months and then MGM was reading it  sort of simultaneously to us and we all  collectively fell in love with it and  MGM made an offer immediately and we  were off to the races  how'd the casting come together guys  well honestly we went straight to Kris  and Kris said yes and then and then we  went out to Oscar so it was it's a weird  one because there's so many times when  there all these bumps in the road but  this was one of these things that  everybody that we talked to was  profoundly passionate about came on  pretty quickly too when you are starting  to look at the stories that are told  around us there are different people who  have written about their recollections  and some of those recollections are at  odds with each other so how do you  figure out what is the core of the truth  I mean this is not quite Rashomon but  people have different memories of  specific incidents so how do you figure  out what is the story and how truthful  you're gonna be to what you believe the  consensus of facts point toward I think  you we tried to be as accurate as  possible and we had various people  keeping us honest not the least an ex  Mossad agent called Avner Avraham who is  the kind of world expert on the mission  and who mounted the exhibition that's  currently during the world about  operation finale that being said people  remember things differently and if and  there are several memoirs which are  mutually contradictory unsurprisingly  each agent felt that he was the hero of  the mission  Peter Malkin was in many ways the most  interesting of these guys and reported  having had these kind of strange  intimate conversations with Eichmann in  the safehouse so that was kind of the  heart of I think Matt's script and I  think the most appealing thing  for Oscar to to perform every detail in  the movie has been vetted in one way or  another so we we extrapolated certain  things we just opposed things we move  things forward in time but it is an  incredibly well informed picture of of  what happened inevitably making a two  hour version of any series of events  amounts to a to taking artistic license  but we were pretty careful about about  what we put in on a production side when  you figure out that you want to and  should be shooting in Argentina what are  the obstacles to doing that and is it  worth noting that there probably still  some people in Argentina whose loyalties  are maybe leaning more toward Eichmann  than they are toward Israel and here in  the US to probably well we'll get to  that in a minute as I think many people  in this room probably can relate to  anytime you have a script that's set in  a foreign country there's someone who  holds the purse strings telling you to  shoot anywhere but that place so we were  we were told that Budapest would be a  great place to make this movie or  Vancouver and it just on paper never  makes sense to shoot with the story set  and as producers and filmmakers it is  always the best choice and we pretty  good at fighting that fight and to  everyone's credit MGM is not you know  most videos are not looking for period  spy dramas so they they were incredibly  emotionally invested in this store and  wanting to tell Italy and as soon as it  became our responsibility to make the  numbers work they supported that  decision 100% and I think you probably  felt in the movie even though there's a  lot of interior conversations that yet  that specificity of place and time is  reflected so powerfully and viscerally  in the movie and I think it just adds a  character to the film that's you know  unreplaceable Jason how much reference  is there for physical locations for what  the houses look like and maybe what  Oscar and Ben were able to use and  of how these people spoke and what they  sounded like how they behaved  I know Oskar used Peter Malkin's book  quite a bit as reference for his  character on the conversations Chris  spoke about our production designer did  an amazing job building the house on  Garibaldi Street and you know finding  all these other locations where her  location scouts were I mean amazing we  shot the movie and had it going up in  Bariloche we did all the Austria stuff  up there and we shot all throughout the  city what the house was hour and a half  outside or we had to schlep every day  for weeks yeah I mean there was every  reason kind of financially to build the  the interrogation room and parse the  safehouse in a studio but we found a  house that felt very much like what we  knew about the the original safehouse  and we figured out that we could still  shoot in sequence within that house so  that sometimes you know there were  lighting arrangements that were left in  place so that that big lights didn't  have to be humped up and down stairs but  but the crew had to move a lot of heavy  equipment up and down stairs in order to  shoot it in sequence oh I thought that  was worth doing I think also there's  some really creepy real things that  happen while we were shooting the night  of the capture that lightning and  thunder was actually happening at that  moment and it was it was very bizarre  night I mean watch all that Eichmann  himself said a couple of obviously very  contradictory things about himself and  about the role he played in the war  toward the end of the war Eichmann said  I'm quoting him now with leap laughing  into the grave because the feeling that  he had five million people on his  conscience would be for him a source of  extraordinary satisfaction and yet when  he was captured and when he was seeking  clemency he said there's a need to draw  a line between the leaders responsible  and the people like me  forced to serve as mere instruments in  the hands of the leader I was not a  responsible leader and as such do not  feel myself guilty yeah that's a lot to  play as an actor  so in your conversations with Ben what  were the things you 